3. MANDATORY MARKING OF MODIFICATIONS

3.1 General requirement. If You make a Modification to pre-existing
material from the Work, You must add a Modification Notice to each
file or section You altered, stating:

  (a) that the file or section was changed from its original form;
  (b) a specific, meaningful summary of the change (a description such
      as "changed stuff" or "misc edits" is insufficient where a more
      specific description can reasonably be given);
  (c) the date the change was made; and
  (d) the identity of the modifier (legal name, organizational
      identity, or a persistent public handle that reasonably
      identifies them — "anonymous" or "someone" is not sufficient
      where the modifier's identity is reasonably known).

3.2 Placement. The Notice must be placed in the file header,
immediately above the altered section, or in an immediately
associated notice file where an embedded notice is not practicable
for the file format. A commit message, changelog, or release note
ALONE does not satisfy this requirement, though it may be used in
addition.

3.3 Multiple alterations / multiple modifiers. A single file-level
Notice may cover multiple altered sections if it clearly identifies
each one; otherwise each section needs its own. Where different
people modify the same file over time, notices may accumulate — each
modifier must still be separately identified with their own date and
summary.

3.4 Timing. The Notice must be added when the Modification is made
and, in all cases, before the modified material is Distributed.

3.5 Scope — what this does NOT cover. This marking requirement
applies ONLY to Modifications of pre-existing material. It does NOT
require a "modified" Notice for a New Original Contribution — material
authored from scratch that contains no copied, adapted, or
incorporated pre-existing expressive material from the Work. A new
filename alone does not exempt copied code: if a "new" file contains a
copied or adapted portion of pre-existing material, THAT portion is a
Modification and must be marked, even though the rest of the new file
may remain a New Original Contribution requiring no notice.

