APPENDIX A — RECOMMENDED MODIFICATION NOTICE TEMPLATES
(Not part of the License itself — provided for convenience.)

For a modified pre-existing file:

    /*
     * CHANGED FROM ORIGINAL
     * Modifier: [NAME / HANDLE] <[CONTACT]>
     * Date: [YYYY-MM-DD]
     * Change Summary: [Specific description of what changed and why.]
     *
     * This file contains modifications to pre-existing material from
     * [PROJECT NAME], Copyright (c) [YEAR] [COPYRIGHT HOLDER],
     * distributed under the [PROJECT NAME] Modification-Notice
     * Permissive Software License, Version 1.0. See LICENSE.
     */

For a section-level modification only:

    // CHANGED FROM ORIGINAL — SECTION: [function/class/section name]
    // Modifier: [NAME] on [YYYY-MM-DD]
    // Change: [Specific description of the change to this section.]

For a New Original Contribution (optional — no notice required):

    /*
     * [FILE NAME]
     * Copyright (c) [YEAR] [YOUR NAME]
     * This is a New Original Contribution to [PROJECT NAME] and
     * contains no modified pre-existing material.
     * [Optional: state the license terms you are applying to this
     * file, if different from the project's default License.]
     */


APPENDIX B — QUICK REFERENCE: MODIFICATION vs. NEW CONTRIBUTION

  Scenario                                          → Requires Notice?
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Editing 3 lines in an existing file                → YES (that file)
  Adding a brand-new file, written from scratch       → NO
  Same commit: edit old file + add new file           → YES for the
                                                          old file only
  Copying a function from an old file into a new one  → YES, for the
                                                          copied portion
                                                          only
  Moving a function from file A to file B              → YES, for BOTH
                                                          files
  Rewriting a function from scratch (new expression,
  same name/purpose/signature)                         → NO
  Reformatting an entire existing file                 → YES

  Rule of thumb: ask whether pre-existing PROTECTED EXPRESSION was
  copied, adapted, or relocated — not whether the filename is new.
